\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.49}{\ignorespaces A Monte Carlo simulation suggests that there is a 50\% probability that 0.5 or more ransomware events will occur within the  sample. This unusual result is because the confidence bounds are between 0 and 1. We interpret this as a 50\% probability that one malware event will occur. \endnote { Sample size was set equivalent to the City of Chattanooga to determine the per capita rates for each year over data from  American Samoa and California. The medians of those rates were used to set upper and lower bounds with  American Samoa and California representing low and high  cybercrime counts respectively.} \endnote {Monte Carlo Modeling Toolkit authored by Derek E. Brink, \gls {cissp}, \url  {www.linkedin.com/in/derekbrink}. March 2023.} }}{166}{figure.caption.188}\protected@file@percent }
